{
  "difficultyNoBombMordor": {
    "_info": {
      "comment": "Mordor's difficulty rule, which is ONE rule for the network's whole history. EIP-2 and EIP-100 are active from genesis and the difficulty bomb never existed here -- DisposalBlock is 0 and ECIP1010PauseBlock is null -- so there is no growing phase, no pause and no removal to assert. What there IS to assert is that nothing happens where mainnet's bomb happens.",
      "theASSERTIONIsTHATNOTHINGMOVES": "The first nine cases in every label hold the interval at 9 seconds, where EIP-100's numerator gives a sigma of exactly zero, so the correct answer is the parent's difficulty UNCHANGED. They then walk the block number across every landmark of mainnet's bomb -- its first period at 200,000, the pause at 3,000,000, the frozen window, the continue block at 5,000,000, and the removal at 5,900,000. All nine must return the same number. A client that ported mainnet's bomb schedule to Mordor produces a different answer at eight of the nine, and the divergence is enormous rather than subtle: the exponential clause at 3,000,000 alone adds 2**28.",
      "whyEveryLabelAgrees": "Mordor's six rule sets differ in EVM behaviour and not in difficulty: EIP-100 arrives at genesis rather than partway through, and no later upgrade touches the adjustment. Six identical columns is therefore the correct answer and is itself an assertion -- on mainnet the same sweep moves three times.",
      "contrastWithMainnet": "../../mainnet/difficulty/bomb_pause_and_removal.json asserts the opposite shape: the same heights give DIFFERENT answers there, because the bomb grows, freezes and vanishes. Reading the two files together is the clearest statement of what ECIP-1041 at block 0 actually means.",
      "oracle": "The production client's own difficulty machinery through its transition tool, which computes currentDifficulty from parentDifficulty, the two timestamps and the parent's uncle hash. Independently cross-checked against a reimplementation of the EIP-100 form across 60 combinations of height, interval and uncle state, with full agreement.",
      "aNoteOnTheFORKNAMESUsed": "The client's state-test fork configurations carry no bomb schedule at all, which is what makes them usable as Mordor's oracle here: at ETC_Atlantis they give the EIP-100 adjustment with no exponential clause at any height, which is exactly Mordor's rule. Do not read the label names as Mordor upgrade names; they name EIP sets.",
      "oracle-version": "core-geth modernized 1.13.0-26975d6a",
      "generated": "2026-08-25",
      "wrongBuildScores": {
        "method": "Scored 2026-08-25 by reimplementing the adjustment and the exponential clause and varying which schedule the bomb follows.",
        "correct": "78/78",
        "bombPresentAndNeverRemoved": "6/78",
        "homesteadDivisor10Instead0f9": "12/78",
        "portsMainnetsBombScheduleToMordor": "18/78 -- the defect this file exists for, and the one a client most plausibly has, since supporting a second ETC network usually means reusing the first one's difficulty path",
        "eip100UncleNumeratorIgnored": "72/78 -- caught by the single uncle-bearing case, which is the only one that can see it",
        "notAboutSubTLETY": "The bomb defect is not a rounding difference. At mainnet's pause block the exponential clause alone adds 2**28, against a parent difficulty of roughly 1.3e7 -- the wrong answer is about twenty times the right one. This file's value is not that the error is hard to see once you look, but that nothing else in the suite looks."
      }
    },
